{"title":"Laurent Series","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>The <b>Laurent series</b> of a complex function <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/1/301469a1d6b6bd4ddb15b26b27e22c03d5611b2e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"32\" height=\"18\" /> is a representation of that function as a power series which includes terms of negative degree. It may be used to express complex functions in cases where a Taylor series expansion cannot be applied. It is named after French mathematician Pierre Alphonse Laurent, who formulated this idea in 1843.\n</p>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Creating_the_Laurent_Series\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Creating the Laurent Series</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#The_First_Theorem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">The First Theorem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#The_Second_Theorem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">The Second Theorem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#The_Third_Theorem_(The_Laurent_Series_Defined)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">The Third Theorem (The Laurent Series Defined)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#The_Fourth_Theorem_(Uniqueness_of_the_Laurent_Series)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">The Fourth Theorem (Uniqueness of the Laurent Series)</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Creating_the_Laurent_Series\">Creating the Laurent Series</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Laurent_Series&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Creating the Laurent Series\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/d/4dd5c3ab23569ef703c31b2e14542a4313c4b620.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0&lt;r_1&lt;|w-z_0|&lt;r_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"177\" height=\"20\" /> such that \n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/c/64ccf7d952b343311500683ee9356b6d3b76e86d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C_m=\\mathcal{C}(t;z_0+r_me^{it},0,2\\pi)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"214\" height=\"22\" /></center>\n<p>for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/2/202fc0afd4383c67dc485f4a9ea05f1bc682f98c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m=1,2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"66\" height=\"15\" />.  In order to create the Laurent Series, we need to prove four main theorems.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"The_First_Theorem\">The First Theorem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Laurent_Series&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: The First Theorem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Suppose that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/9/f896dac28297634e655ef35416dd97c775ffeaa0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(w)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"37\" height=\"18\" /> is holomorphic for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/7/9b7d598b8c19ca99454dff822146fd514605d729.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_1\\le |z-z_0|\\le r_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"139\" height=\"20\" />.  Then\n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/1/10160932b89662a63ecd07d52a485104cb3d3a26.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\pi f(w)=\\oint_{C_2}\\frac{f(z)}{z-w}\\,dz-\\oint_{C_1}\\frac{f(z)}{z-w}\\,dz$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"308\" height=\"46\" />.</center>\n<p>The proof is actually not terrible.  Consider some complex valued function <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/1/311cabda3a9b09f0dde217303ca9d1cd9201dcf6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$g$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> such that\n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/6/966d224fe58999b3fbbbaacd0394e383313df7ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$g(z)=\\frac{f(z)-f(w)}{z-w}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"158\" height=\"42\" /></center>\n<p>where this exists, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/c/d6ce30d14119e72c3028a3d887a30d5c4adbada8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$g(w)=f&#39;(w)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"106\" height=\"20\" />.  Then it follows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/6/25644550d7cac6c96494ec66376348d1a1f431f6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$g(z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"31\" height=\"18\" /> is holomorphic for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/7/9b7d598b8c19ca99454dff822146fd514605d729.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_1\\le |z-z_0|\\le r_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"139\" height=\"20\" />.  Hence,\n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/6/b868e59eade21c5f7a3a5aff845b713e097c5eb7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\oint_{C_1}g(z)\\,dz=\\oint_{C_2}g(z)\\,dz$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"194\" height=\"44\" />.</center>\n<p>Now, notice that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/c/62ca011f5f4c68333dcb042af6cc97f752cad031.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{f(z)}{z-w}=g(z)+\\frac{f(w)}{z-w}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"179\" height=\"42\" /> for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/1/9a14e8ee37b5d13d33b96e2c69bd187d09f5eec4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z\\in C_1,C_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"82\" height=\"18\" />.  This means we get\n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/e/83e2ed2a5bdf6b08d2636e9d95470d7602dad956.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\oint_{C_2}\\frac{f(z)}{z-w}\\,dz-\\oint_{C_1}\\frac{f(z)}{z-w}=f(w)\\left(\\oint_{C_2}\\frac{1}{z-w}\\,dz-\\oint_{C_1}\\frac{1}{z-w}\\right).$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"505\" height=\"46\" /></center>\n<p>Clearly we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/f/c8f6f749eec59130e76b5433572fb7c328510378.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w\\in\\text{Ext}(C_1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"99\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/9/a498190ea51e765a380e91ebae4188c259593349.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w\\in\\text{Int}(C_2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"93\" height=\"20\" /> which means that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/7/db7ac205081792098e3f57a03b831638eb53297a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\oint_{C_2}\\frac{1}{z-w}\\,dz=2\\pi i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"153\" height=\"44\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/d/7ddd33b5ff689ebe6d7c526b4e315381aa8df021.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\oint_{C_1}\\frac{1}{z-w}\\,dz=0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"136\" height=\"44\" /> by a theorem.  This implies the result <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/c/b6cf65b1f2fbdf388e3daeff9b96b34d3399d777.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\square$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"The_Second_Theorem\">The Second Theorem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Laurent_Series&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: The Second Theorem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Suppose that the first theorem holds and let\n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/b/1eb92299ef183adb6ec3f0d56b1d7bc4253b3998.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_n=\\frac{1}{2\\pi i}\\oint_{C_m}\\frac{f(z)}{(z-z_0)^{n+1}}\\,dz$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"225\" height=\"46\" /></center>\n<p>for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/3/463369a836bf5959c9986514bdb89aad2f0a07b3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n\\in\\mathbb{Z}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"13\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/2/202fc0afd4383c67dc485f4a9ea05f1bc682f98c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m=1,2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"66\" height=\"15\" /> as long as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/3/9238d82d59d5dfe79e96d916bfdf2f4acc4ed3be.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n\\ge 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/8/79813fe3de65c5970b8c1b6eecc359c0042447ae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n&lt;0$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"13\" />.  We claim that\n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/8/fa8a906a8c32eaaf1f3762ef3b166a759b605b0d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(w)=\\sum_{n=-\\infty}^{\\infty}a_n(w-z_0)^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -24px\" width=\"203\" height=\"54\" />.</center>\n<p>Note that from before, we have\n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/1/591c63fbf56b80e358db67700918d50daecb1d77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{z-w}=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{w-z_0)^n}{(z-z_0)^{n+1}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"195\" height=\"53\" /></center>\n<p>for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/f/33f2e4607ff0dff5ee25ec4c303a117083b32c10.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z\\in|C_2|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"64\" height=\"20\" />.  Now, there must exist an <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/c/d7cc76f94e1a2dbbc8bd0598653c458690d95cd3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(z)\\le M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"80\" height=\"20\" /> for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/f/33f2e4607ff0dff5ee25ec4c303a117083b32c10.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z\\in|C_2|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"64\" height=\"20\" /> by a theorem, so we get the inequality\n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/7/407bfa79ae22d82c360c58e33c2c01246aaf1c0f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{f(z)(w-z_0)^n}{(z-z_0)^{n+1}}\\le \\frac{M|w-z_0|^n}{r_2^{n+1}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"234\" height=\"45\" /></center>\n<p>for each <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/f/33f2e4607ff0dff5ee25ec4c303a117083b32c10.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z\\in|C_2|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"64\" height=\"20\" /> once again.  But notice: the right hand side is independent of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />! So, because we already assumed that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/a/07ae6b78d40753198e6e05be515839c9e5300701.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_1&lt;|w-z_0|&lt;r_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"143\" height=\"20\" /> we see that the sum\n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/7/a37f498c4e46fb849cca65d64ddffe55813d9867.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{M|w-z_0|^n}{r_2^{n+1}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"125\" height=\"53\" /></center>\n<p>converges.  Hence, the sum\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/8/fb89a85a5f82dde68b9b96cadb91825d6fa7a3fb.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{f(z)(w-z_0)^n}{(z-z_0)^{n+1}}\\]\" width=\"140\" height=\"48\" />\nconverges uniformly on the contour <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/5/b65e6facfe122c182fe14d503aeace2c52a9739d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" />.  This means we get\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/5/3551e3500eedd5a2520f6c8b76bdbe6a039c1fb8.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} \\oint_{C_2}\\frac{f(z)}{z-w}\\,dz&amp;=\\oint_{C_2}\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{f(z)(w-z_0)^n}{(z-z_0)^{n+1}}\\,dz\\\\ &amp;=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}(w-z_0)^n\\oint_{C_2}\\frac{f(z)}{(z-z_0)^{n+1}}\\,dz\\\\ &amp;=2\\pi i\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}(w-z_0)^n. \\end{align*}\" width=\"369\" height=\"163\" />\nBut we are not finished just yet! Also notice that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/b/97bfa992d8f9dcfaf2008c9cce32e4343c3524d5.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} \\frac{1}{w-z}&amp;=\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{(z-z_0)^{n-1}}{(w-z_0)^n}\\\\ &amp;=\\sum_{n=-\\infty}^{-1}\\frac{(w-z_0)^n}{(z-z_0)^{n+1}} \\end{align*}\" width=\"206\" height=\"108\" />\nfor each <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/a/7aa5217fee60a0270547db5e4ead25b9527e77c7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z\\in|C_1|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"64\" height=\"20\" />.  This gives\n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/6/ff6397d7c2a7d7757cdbf2d803cee269da8c673d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-\\oint_{C_1}\\frac{f(z)}{z-w}\\,dz=2\\pi i\\sum_{n=-\\infty}^{-1}\\frac{(w-z_0)^n}{(z-z_0)^{n+1}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -24px\" width=\"309\" height=\"57\" /></center>\n<p>and rearrangement gives the desired <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/c/b6cf65b1f2fbdf388e3daeff9b96b34d3399d777.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\square$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p>\n<h3><span id=\"The_Third_Theorem_.28The_Laurent_Series_Defined.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"The_Third_Theorem_(The_Laurent_Series_Defined)\">The Third Theorem (The Laurent Series Defined)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Laurent_Series&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: The Third Theorem (The Laurent Series Defined)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>We define the term \"a ring in a wider sense\" to mean the following: the set of points between two concentric circles, a disk without its center, and the exterior of a circle not including <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/7/b671f1bb7e4ee86584347d5d22f1dc8abdb5bef2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\infty$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"17\" height=\"9\" />.  Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/b/f2b686e282112ee7338ebaf3427727ac8141c10f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{R}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"15\" height=\"13\" /> be a ring in the wider sense, with center <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/b/bab9abe98679312d2a3308c76188cf2cbc492f68.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" /> and let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> be holomorphic on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/b/f2b686e282112ee7338ebaf3427727ac8141c10f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{R}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"15\" height=\"13\" />.  Then there are numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/2/412787c048e28774dc63fc27db42dc52ca858de7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" /> for each <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/3/e6304c4849872102b9e25f43f363b4bf81a16982.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i\\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"103\" height=\"20\" /> such that for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/4/064d980e2e8b578b2a00f4629c9a5d4a1b066680.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w\\in \\mathcal{R}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"53\" height=\"15\" /> we have the series\n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/8/fa8a906a8c32eaaf1f3762ef3b166a759b605b0d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(w)=\\sum_{n=-\\infty}^{\\infty}a_n(w-z_0)^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -24px\" width=\"203\" height=\"54\" />.</center>\n<p>Choose any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/3/8b3a2616732fe5edb81142978f78b16652df7035.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r&gt;0$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"41\" height=\"13\" /> such that the circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/f/f4faea352ba8a4e8df856b87a7972f057446acd1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C=\\mathcal{C}(t;z_0+r_me^{it},0,2\\pi)\\in\\mathcal{R}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"241\" height=\"22\" /> and let\n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/c/e2ccfeed36daf76766ba201a225dd9768024ae10.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_n=\\frac{1}{2\\pi i}\\oint_{C}\\frac{f(z)}{(z-z_0)^{n+1}}\\,dz$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"215\" height=\"45\" /></center>\n<p>for every <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/3/463369a836bf5959c9986514bdb89aad2f0a07b3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n\\in\\mathbb{Z}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"13\" />.  Choose some <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/f/e1faee560ef56f7ca9c7c336396c940f20c945a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w\\in\\mathcal{R}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"53\" height=\"15\" />, and let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/c/adc143c3f887fa7e92fd80d91bb22af51b4abd77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"17\" height=\"13\" />,<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/4/0b4f3f4b698ce35667432041e168f7653533fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"17\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/b/70b97eb4ad6e4e7a5a97e2547d9ed6e965960d0c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/5/b65e6facfe122c182fe14d503aeace2c52a9739d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> denote the same stuff we used earlier.  Then it follows that\n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/0/b20b1ede7748f4c4eedb912ffb412cb458217974.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\oint_{C}\\frac{f(z)}{(z-z_0)^{n+1}}\\,dz=\\oint_{C_m}\\frac{f(z)}{(z-z_0)^{n+1}}\\,dz$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"311\" height=\"46\" /></center>\n<p>for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/2/202fc0afd4383c67dc485f4a9ea05f1bc682f98c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m=1,2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"66\" height=\"15\" /> of course.  The theorem then follows immediately <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/c/b6cf65b1f2fbdf388e3daeff9b96b34d3399d777.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\square$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> \n</p>\n<h3><span id=\"The_Fourth_Theorem_.28Uniqueness_of_the_Laurent_Series.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"The_Fourth_Theorem_(Uniqueness_of_the_Laurent_Series)\">The Fourth Theorem (Uniqueness of the Laurent Series)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Laurent_Series&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: The Fourth Theorem (Uniqueness of the Laurent Series)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Is such a series unique, however? It certainly should be, or else I would not be writing this all down here.  Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/b/f2b686e282112ee7338ebaf3427727ac8141c10f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{R}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"15\" height=\"13\" /> be a ring in the wider sense with center <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/b/bab9abe98679312d2a3308c76188cf2cbc492f68.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" /> of a circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/6/5/865f4b2b7a6ab1ba84c85d9696332672484e536c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C\\in\\mathcal{R}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"54\" height=\"15\" />, and let\n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff54078174f88ea5dda00268c2b123fe3782a241.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(z)=\\sum_{n=-\\infty}^{\\infty}a_n(z-z_0)^m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -24px\" width=\"198\" height=\"54\" /></center>\n<p>for every <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/8/ba81fc5713e4f3f849b9f98b99f53ac911ebaa08.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z\\in\\mathcal{R}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"49\" height=\"15\" />.  Then we must prove that\n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/0/a/c0aeeabb745d8490a198a7105f9dc27477590efa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_n=\\frac{1}{2\\pi i}\\oint_{C}\\frac{f(z)}{(z-z_0)^{m+1}}\\,dz$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"219\" height=\"45\" /></center>\n<p>holds for every integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" />.  We have\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/e/00ed09e729380e392d9bc7472f6effed4b8628f9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} \\oint_{C}\\frac{f(z)}{(z-z_0)^{m+1}}\\,dz&amp;=\\oint_{C}\\sum_{n=-\\infty}^{\\infty}a_n(z-z_0)^{n-m-1}\\,dz.\\\\ \\end{align*}\" width=\"387\" height=\"48\" />\nTo swap the integrand and the sum, we must note that\n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/2/6d2dd14e28f56ee079c1b541e040ceaf918b1f83.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{n=-\\infty}^{\\infty}c_n=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}c_n+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}c_{-n}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -24px\" width=\"217\" height=\"54\" /></center>\n<p>Now we can have the following deduction.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/4/f94ff61fdd822f4963c78dbe3bf8ca1994487984.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} \\oint_{C}\\frac{f(z)}{(z-z_0)^{m+1}}\\,dz&amp;=\\oint_{C}\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}a_n(z-z_0)^{n-m-1}\\,dz+\\oint_{C}\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}a_{-n}(z-z_0)^{n-m-1}\\,dz\\\\ &amp;=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}a_n\\oint_{C}(z-z_0)^{n-m-1}\\,dz+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}a_{-n}\\oint_{C}(z-z_0)^{n-m-1}\\,dz\\\\ &amp;=\\sum_{n=-\\infty}^{\\infty}a_n\\oint_{C}(z-z_0)^{n-m-1}\\,dz.\\\\ \\end{align*}\" width=\"602\" height=\"163\" />\nCrisis avoided! Sort of.  We assumed that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/6/b461e970b352c09dda1868c8ea93d666b85e3e4c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}a_n(z-z_0)^{n-m-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"159\" height=\"53\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/0/6606d0d29418dc34abca56a510d42030adcbe607.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}a_{-n}(z-z_0)^{n-m-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"169\" height=\"53\" /> converge uniformly on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />.  We can fix this! Let the radius of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> (remember, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is a circle) be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />.  Then there exists some <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/a/9ea31e5c5613f025b5be8aca425d318168491d89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/f/8ff9798a813db043817dfd8f2fcb648f0b3731bb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|z_1-z_0|&gt;r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"99\" height=\"20\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/4/5146b19eb703b27af96f2e2e3220fa8ae8c659fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z_1\\in\\mathcal{R}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"55\" height=\"17\" />.  It follows that the series \n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff54078174f88ea5dda00268c2b123fe3782a241.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(z)=\\sum_{n=-\\infty}^{\\infty}a_n(z-z_0)^m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -24px\" width=\"198\" height=\"54\" /></center>\n<p>holds when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/9/529820d72609ead9c8431f728683884acd546d46.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z_1=z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"48\" height=\"10\" /> so we get\n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/b/54b5ec08254136045942b55b87a3c9fc963f01ab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(z)=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}a_n(z-z_0)^m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"182\" height=\"53\" /></center>\n<p>converges.  We have to prove uniform convergence though, so back to work.  Notice that this result above implies that there is some <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/b/fbb4c610907d85a238a377fc991911bfea9a0c44.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|a_n(z-z_0)^n|\\le k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"135\" height=\"20\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/1/9013bb191380c197d4fbea9ef176b90631bde0bf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n\\ge0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"15\" />.  Hence we get\n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/4/db4171a49654bab189a11cef3c71d995d704f386.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|a_n(z-z_0)^{n-m-1}|\\le kr^{-m-1}\\left(\\frac{r}{|z-z_0|}\\right)^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"323\" height=\"46\" /></center>\n<p>for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/3/0637c9e65b3b850b022c922b771438220feac1d1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z\\in|C|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"58\" height=\"20\" /> and where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/1/9013bb191380c197d4fbea9ef176b90631bde0bf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n\\ge0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"15\" />.  Like before, the RHS is independent of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> so we get\n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/0/b90eef1d4947ea1a3a28eb20d7d9a3bf48aa9048.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}kr^{-m-1}\\left(\\frac{r}{|z-z_0|}\\right)^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"189\" height=\"53\" /></center>\n<p>converges, which implies that \n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/b/54b5ec08254136045942b55b87a3c9fc963f01ab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(z)=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}a_n(z-z_0)^m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"182\" height=\"53\" /></center>\n<p>converges uniformly.  Repeat the same process for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/0/6606d0d29418dc34abca56a510d42030adcbe607.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}a_{-n}(z-z_0)^{n-m-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"169\" height=\"53\" /> with appropriate tweaks.  Now, we finish by noting that\n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/7/c7782f555f7ed4525123027b4bf609012971a854.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\oint_{C}(z-z_0)^{n-m-1}\\,dz=2\\pi i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"205\" height=\"42\" /></center>\n<p>when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/c/fec4f3da4f422b2346efe86724c8a0aee1023fac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m=n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"53\" height=\"10\" /> or we have\n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/b/adb50275dee5b67c4f1af6ebdf89db07adb5969f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\oint_{C}(z-z_0)^{n-m-1}\\,dz=0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"188\" height=\"42\" /></center>\n<p>when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/8/648235a71039bea6360ae73859772e4c126a4ee6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m\\ne n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"50\" height=\"17\" />.  The result follows from \n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/6/c96f4e154126a9235327980e054d2b04438b783c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{n=-\\infty}^{\\infty}a_n\\oint_{C}(z-z_0)^{n-m-1}\\,dz$\" style=\"vertical-align: -24px\" width=\"221\" height=\"54\" />.</center>\n<p>This shows that the series is unique, done <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/c/b6cf65b1f2fbdf388e3daeff9b96b34d3399d777.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\square$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018002726\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.035 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.045 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 454/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 20633/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:22647:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018002726 and revision id 282834. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}